@c page
@node overview
@chapter Overview of the package


This document describes version @value{VERSION} of @value{PACKAGE}, a
modern embeddable key--value database designed for a high load
environment.

It has a unique architecture that was born as a result of research and
rethinking primary alghorithmical constraints associated with a getting
popular Log--file based data structures, such as LSM--tree, its
variations based on Fractional Cascading ideas and a B-Tree.

It is written entirely in C and can be used in C/C++ applications or by
any interpreted language if the appropriate library bindings are
available.

Sophia supports the traditional set/get/delete operations semantic plus
range queries by using cursors.  Database @api{} has been designed to be
as clean as possible and easy to use.

All sophia @api{} declarations are stored in a separate include file:
@file{sophia.h}.

And the library to link with is @code{-lsophia} accordingly.

@c ------------------------------------------------------------

@subsubheading Conventions

All functions return either @code{0} on success, or @code{-1} on error.
The only exception is functions that return a pointer; in that case
@cnull{} indicates an error.

Any error description can be accessed through @cfunc{sp_error} function.

All created objects must be freed by @cfunc{sp_destroy} function.  The
only exception is the @cfunc{sp_get} function, which allocates the
returned value via @cfunc{malloc} (and should be freed using
@cfunc{free} accordingly) or using the allocator--free function
specified in @cfunc{sp_ctl}.

@c ------------------------------------------------------------

@subsubheading Database

To open or create a database the @cfunc{sp_open} function must be used
with a configuration object (environment) previously created using
@cfunc{sp_env}.  The @cfunc{sp_env} function must be intialized using
@cfunc{sp_ctl}.  Before opening, a database directory must be specified
by @cfunc{sp_ctl} with flag @code{SPDIR}.

@example
#include <sophia.h>
void *env = sp_env();
if (env == NULL) @{
        /* memory allocation error */
@}
int rc = sp_ctl(env, SPDIR, SPO_CREAT|SPO_RDWR, "./db");
if (rc == -1) @{
        /* memory allocation error */
        printf("error: %s\n", sp_error(env));
        sp_destroy(env);
@}
@end example

To create a database directory the @code{SPO_CREAT} flag must be passed
to the @cfunc{sp_ctl} function.

The @cfunc{sp_open} function allocates a database handle and does the
main job of creating a new or recovering an existing database.

@example
void *db = sp_open(env);
if (db == NULL) @{
        printf("error: %s\n", sp_error(env));
        sp_destroy(env);
@}
@end example

@c ------------------------------------------------------------

@subsubheading Operations

There are a number of operations available to write or read data.  All
functions accept first arguments as key and key size.

To insert a new key--value pair use @cfunc{sp_set}.

@example
char value[] = "hello world";
int key = 0;
while (key < 10) @{
        rc = sp_set(db, &key, sizeof(key), value, sizeof(value));
        if (rc == -1) @{
                printf("sp_set: %s\n", sp_error(db));
                break;
        @}
        key++;
@}
@end example

To get a key use @cfunc{sp_get}.

@cfunc{sp_get} allocates a value, which should be freed afterwards using
the @cfunc{free} function (or by allocator specific function passed to
@cfunc{sp_ctl} with flag @code{SPALLOC}.

@example
int key = 0;
while (key < 10) @{
        void *value;
        size_t valuesize;
        int rc = sp_get(s, &key, sizeof(key), &value, &valuesize);
        if (rc == -1) @{
                printf("sp_get: %s\n", sp_error(db));
                break;
        @}
        printf("key: %d, value: %s\n", key, value);
        free(value);
        key++;
@}
@end example

Please note that it is much faster to use range queries for sequential
key reads.

To delete a key use @cfunc{sp_delete}.

@example
int key = 0;
while (key < 10) @{
        rc = sp_delete(s, &key, sizeof(key));
        if (rc == -1) @{
                printf("sp_delete: %s\n", sp_error(db));
                break;
        @}
        key++;
@}
@end example

@c ------------------------------------------------------------

@subsubheading Range queries

It is possible to do range queries using cursors.

To create a cursor the @cfunc{sp_cursor} function should be used.
Cursor creation function allows to specify a query order and a start
key.

Possible orders are:

@table @code
@item SPGTE
Increasing order, including supplied key.

@item SPGT
Increasing order, excluding supplied key.

@item SPLTE
Decreasing order, including supplied key.

@item SPLT
Decreasing order, excluding supplied key.
@end table

If no key is specified whole key range will be traversed.

@example
void *c = sp_cursor(db, SPGTE, NULL, 0);
if (c == NULL) @{
        printf("cursor failed: %s\n", sp_error(db));
        /* error */
@}
@end example

To do a single step of iteration @cfunc{sp_fetch} function should be
used.  It returns @code{1} if there is more data available and @code{0}
otherwise:

@example
while (sp_fetch(c)) @{
        char *k = sp_key(c);
        char *v = sp_value(c);
        printf("key: %d, value: %s\n", *(int*)k, v);
@}
@end example

To get current key or value data, @cfunc{sp_key}, @cfunc{sp_keysize} and
@cfunc{sp_value}, @cfunc{sp_valuesize} functions should be used
accordingly.

Cursor objects should be freed using the @cfunc{sp_destroy} function
after a usage.

@c ------------------------------------------------------------

@subsubheading Example

@example
#include <sophia.h>
int main(void)
@{
        /* create environment handle */
        void *env = sp_env();
        if (env == NULL)
                return 0;
        /* set database directory with it's open flags */
        int rc = sp_ctl(env, SPDIR, SPO_CREAT|SPO_RDWR, "./db");
        if (rc == -1) @{
                sp_destroy(env);
                return 0;
        @}
        /* open a existing or create the new database */
        void *db = sp_open(env);
        if (db == NULL) @{
                printf("error: %s\n", sp_error(env));
                sp_destroy(env);
                return 0;
        @}
        /* sequentially insert key-value pairs */
        char value[] = "hello world";
        int key = 0;
        while (key < 10) @{
                rc = sp_set(s, &key, sizeof(key), value, sizeof(value));
                if (rc == -1) @{
                        printf("sp_set: %s\n", sp_error(db));
                        goto err;
                @}
                key++;
        @}
        /* sequentially get key values.
         *
         * By default, order is defined by memcmp(3) function in
         * standart comparator. It is possible to specify custom comparator
         * in sp_ctl(SPCMP).
         */
        key = 0;
        while (key < 10) @{
                void *v;
                size_t vsize;
                rc = sp_get(s, &key, sizeof(key), &v, &vsize);
                if (rc == -1) @{
                        printf("sp_get: %s\n", sp_error(db));
                        goto err;
                @}
                printf("key: %d, value: %s\n", i, v);
                free(v);
                key++;
        @}
err:
        sp_destroy(db);
        sp_destroy(env);
        return 0;
@}
@end example

